y.\r\n- Keep the code editor, explanation, and variable inspector organized.\r\n- Collapse the full execution history by default.\r\n- Hide advanced debugging details in Beginner Mode while preserving Advanced View.\r\n- Fix raw HTML appearing in explanations, clipped text, overflow, and scrolling bugs.\r\n- Ensure the layout works on laptop and mobile screens.\r\n\r\n**Phase 2 — Improve teaching**\r\n- Explain one small operation at a time using simple English.\r\n- Show what happens, why it happens, and what happens next.\r\n- Show calculations using actual variable values.\r\n- Explain conditions using their actual operands and True/False results.\r\n- Explain loop iterations, variable changes, and function returns.\r\n- Keep teaching substeps synchronized with real execution.\r\n\r\n**Phase 3 — Add meaningful animations**\r\n- Animate numbers entering and changing variable boxes.\r\n- Visually demonstrate arithmetic calculations.\r\n- Highlight the active code line.\r\n- Animate condition branches and loop transitions.\r\n- Show variable changes clearly.\r\n- Add animation speed and reduced-motion controls.\r\n- Keep animations short, smooth, optional, and synchronized with the real execution engine. Never fabricate execution results.\r\n\r\n**Phase 4 — Add interactive learning**\r\n- Optionally ask learners to predict a variable value or condition result.\r\n- Reveal the correct answer with a simple explanation.\r\n- Preserve Previous Step, Next Step, Auto Play, Restart, and execution history.\r\n\r\n**Phase 5 — Test everything**\r\n- Verify all existing features still work.\r\n- Test the maximum-subarray example and confirm its output remains 6 for the original input.\r\n- Verify all variable values, conditions, loop iterations, and backward/forward navigation.\r\n- Check for UI bugs, incorrect explanations, and animation synchronization issues.\r\n\r\n**Implementation rules:**\r\n1. Inspect the existing project before editing.\r\n2. Implement each phase sequentially; do not make a huge rewrite.\r\n3. Preserve existing code and working functionality.\r\n4. Reuse existing components and dependencies wherever possible.\r\n5. Test each phase before continuing.\r\n6. Do not claim a feature works unless verified.\r\n7. At completion, report changed files, tests performed, and remaining issues.\r\n\r\nStart with Phase 1 and continue through the phases in order. Prioritize correctness, simplicity, and learning over decorative effects. The final application should feel like a personal coding teacher, not a complicated debugging dashboard.\n\nAct as a senior software engineer and educational UI/UX designer. Improve my existing \"Teach Me This Code\" application so complete beginners can easily understand Python execution.\n\nCRITICAL: Do not rebuild from scratch. Do not modify my original Python code or replace my existing execution engine. Preserve all working features, actual execution values, navigation, and teaching substeps. Make minimal, safe changes to the existing project. No AI APIs or paid services.\n\nImplement these phases in order:\n\nPhase 1 — Fix the interface\n\nCreate a clean, modern, beginner-friendly layout.\n\nMake the teaching area the main focus.\n\nImprove text size, spacing, alignment, and readability.\n\nKeep the code editor, explanation, and variable inspector organized.\n\nCollapse the full execution history by default.\n\nHide advanced debugging details in Beginner Mode while preserving Advanced View.\n\nFix raw HTML appearing in explanations, clipped text, overflow, and scrolling bugs.\n\nEnsure the layout works on laptop and mobile screens.\n\n5. Phase 2 — Improve teaching\n\nExplain one small operation at a time using simple English.\n\nShow what happens, why it happens, and what happens next.\n\nShow calculations using actual variable values.\n\nExplain conditions using their actual operands and True/False results.\n\nExplain loop iterations, variable changes, and function returns.\n\nKeep teaching